{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789913713,"nonce":"6tNlw8pPla0UbPCTXByd+enDNmI=","title":"Compiler: scalar replacement of non-escaping reference allocations","message":"mandelbrot makes a Complex per iteration, op_overload a Vec3 per addition, any_field a one-field table per step. With shapes these are three-word struct allocations, one per iteration; LuaJIT sinks them and PUC Lua does not, which is the 4x between the two on those kernels. Beating LuaJIT needs the allocation gone.\n\nA HIR pass: an allocation whose every use is a field load or store, or a pass to an inlined callee that does the same, and that never reaches a call, a store into memory, a return or a phi with an escaping value, is replaced by one SSA value per field. Runs after inlining and before the release analysis, which must then see no allocation. Earlier note in memory: HIR-level SROA was a dead end on value-type round trips (Cranelift's mem2reg covers those); the target here is reference-type allocations, which no backend removes.","files":null}]}